1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
int a[3][4] holds twelve ints in one block. If a were a pointer to a pointer, sizeof(a) would be the size of one pointer.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
sizeof a is 48, sizeof a[0] is 16, sizeof a[0][0] is 4. Three levels, three sizes, all measured.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
A pointer is one word. On this page a pointer is 8 bytes, so 48 against 8 is not a rounding difference.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
So a is an object of 48 bytes. Everything else in this lesson is that sentence, applied carefully.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Read the declarator from the name outwards: a is an array of 3 things, each of which is an array of 4 ints.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
So a[0], a[1] and a[2] are not ints. Each is a complete int[4], sixteen bytes, and each is a legal array in its own right.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The twelve ints are laid out row by row in one block: 1000 to 1047, with a[2][3] the last at 1044.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
No row pointer is stored anywhere. The geometry is in the type, not in memory.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
In an expression an array becomes a pointer to its first element. The first element of a is a[0], an int[4].

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
So a decays to int (*)[4]: a pointer to an array of four ints. That is read "pointer to", not "array of pointers".

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
One level down: a[0] is an int[4], so it decays to int *. Two levels down: a[0][0] is an int, and an int does not decay.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Three places where no conversion happens: the operand of sizeof, the operand of unary &, and a string literal initialising an array.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
a, &a[0] and &a all hold the number 1000. Three expressions, one address, three different types.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
a + 1 is 1016: one row of sixteen bytes. a[0] + 1 is 1004: one int. &a + 1 is 1048: the whole object.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The general form: a + i moves i times 16, and *(a + i) is the row itself, which then decays to int *.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
&a + 1 is one past the end of the whole object. Forming it is legal; dereferencing it is not.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
The standard defines E1[E2] as *((E1) + (E2)). So a[i][j] is *(*(a + i) + j), and nothing else.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
First expansion: *(a + i) is row i. It has type int[4], so in this expression it decays again, to int *.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Second expansion: adding j moves j ints, and the final dereference is the value. Checked on all twelve cells: both spellings give the same byte.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The row-major formula falls out of the type: base + (i * C + j) * w, with C the number of columns, not the number of rows.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Now read the same 48 bytes as an int **. The first eight bytes are a[0][0] and a[0][1], which are the integers 1000 and 1004.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
On a little-endian 64-bit machine that pair reads as 0x000003ec000003e8 — a number, but not the address of anything in this program.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The compiler rejects int ** p = a; because the types differ. A cast silences the compiler and changes nothing about the bytes.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
What would make int ** correct: an actual array of pointers, filled with real addresses. That is a different object with different contents.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A parameter declared as an array is adjusted to a pointer to its element type. int a[3][4] becomes int (*a)[4].

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Only the outermost dimension is discarded. The inner ones must survive, because they are the row width the arithmetic needs.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
So int a[3][4], int a[100][4] and int a[][4] are three spellings of one parameter type. The 3 and the 100 mean nothing.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
But int a[][] does not compile: it would be an array whose elements are incomplete arrays, and an element type may not be incomplete.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
One asterisk, two types. int (*p)[4] is a pointer to an array of four. int *q[4] is an array of four pointers.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
sizeof tells them apart at once: 8 for the pointer, 32 for the array of four pointers. And p + 1 moves 16 bytes against q + 1 at 8.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
The ragged array int *rows[3] is the array-of-pointers side: three pointers, each free to aim at a row of a different length.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
And rows, being an array, decays to int ** — which is why int ** is right for ragged tables and wrong for int a[3][4].

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Question shape one: int a[5][6], base 1000, four bytes each, row-major. Address of a[3][4] is 1000 + (3*6 + 4)*4 = 1088.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Column-major swaps the roles: 1000 + (4*5 + 3)*4 = 1092. Same array, same cell, four bytes apart.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Question shape two runs backwards: given a[2][3] at 1112 and a[3][1] at 1124 on int a[4][5], the gap is 3 elements, and the base is 1060.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The check that costs nothing: recompute the second given cell from the recovered base. If it does not come back, the base is wrong.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step 1: name the object. Write the type out as array of ... of T before you do anything else.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step 2: ask what the expression has become. Pointer to the element type — and the element type is one level in, not T.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step 3: scale by the pointed-to type, then add. Row width for a pointer to a row, element width for a pointer to an element.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Step 4: check the type of the answer. If a question asks for bytes, say bytes; if it asks for an element index, do not multiply by the width.

